  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/1502.06032v1</id>
    <title>A note on the shameful conjecture</title>
    <updated>2015-02-20T23:05:24Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.06032v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1502.06032v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Let $P_G(q)$ denote the chromatic polynomial of a graph $G$ on $n$ vertices. The `shameful conjecture' due to Bartels and Welsh states that, $$\frac{P_G(n)}{P_G(n-1)} \geq \frac{n^n}{(n-1)^n}.$$ Let $μ(G)$ denote the expected number of colors used in a uniformly random proper $n$-coloring of $G$. The above inequality can be interpreted as saying that $μ(G) \geq μ(O_n)$, where $O_n$ is the empty graph on $n$ nodes. This conjecture was proved by F. M. Dong, who in fact showed that, $$\frac{P_G(q)}{P_G(q-1)} \geq \frac{q^n}{(q-1)^n}$$ for all $q \geq n$. There are examples showing that this inequality is not true for all $q \geq 2$. In this paper, we show that the above inequality holds for all $q \geq 36D^{3/2}$, where $D$ is the largest degree of $G$. It is also shown that the above inequality holds true for all $q \geq 2$ when $G$ is a claw-free graph.</summary>
    <category term="math.CO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2015-02-20T23:05:24Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Accepted to the European Journal of Combinatorics</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="math.CO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Sukhada Fadnavis</name>
    </author>
